--- b4 medium.en: Medigap, officially called Medicare Supplement Insurance, is extra insurance that you buy from a private company. Original Medicare, which is Part A and Part B, covers hospital and doctor costs, but you still owe deductibles, coinsurance, and copayments. Medigap helps pay your share of those costs, and Medicare itself does not pay anything toward the cost of buying a Medigap policy.
--- b4 large-v3: Medigap, officially called Medicare Supplement Insurance, is extra insurance that you buy from a private company. Original Medicare, which is Part A and Part B, covers hospital and doctor costs. But you still owe deductibles, coinsurance, and copayments. Medigap helps pay your share of those costs. And Medicare itself does not pay anything toward the cost of buying a Medigap policy.
--- b5 medium.en: Medigap plans are standardized. In most states, they are named by letters A, B, C, D, F, G, K, L, M, and N. All plans with the same letter offer the same basic benefits, no matter which company sells them. Massachusetts, Minnesota, and Wisconsin are the exceptions. The only difference between two Plan G policies is the price, so compare Plan G with Plan G, not with Plan N.
--- b5 large-v3: Medigap plans are standardized. In most states, they are named by letters. A, B, C, D, F, G, K, L, M, and N. All plans with the same letter offer the same basic benefits, no matter which company sells them. Massachusetts, Minnesota, and Wisconsin are the exceptions. The only difference between two Plan G policies is the price, so compare Plan G with Plan G, not with Plan N.
--- b6 medium.en: Let us look at Plan G. It covers Part A co-insurance and hospital costs, up to an additional 365 days after Medicare benefits are used. It covers Part B co-insurance or co-payment, the first three pints of blood, hospice co-insurance, skilled nursing facility co-insurance, the Part A deductible, and Part B excess charges. Foreign travel emergency care is covered at 80%.
